ssador  Michael McFaul  to a u.s. adversary like Russia is that  normal I don't know if that's normal I  would say I'm adamantly opposed to that  and I don't know of a case in the past  on that through all the history of it  but there's no way I would turn over a  former diplomat  to Russia's for investigation and for  conversation on that they're acting  their official duties we need to  continue to be able to stand by our  folks do you think President Trump is in  some way compromised by Vladimir Putin  no I don't think so at all I've seen no  evidence of that and we've gone through  extensive work in the Senate  intelligence community to be able to go  through a lot of evidence  and a bipartisan way I don't think it  was a compromise there I think he does  treat the president of China and the  president of Russia a little different  than he treats any other world leader  that would that be for economics or just  the complexity of the relationship I  don't know but those two he does treat  different than others but I don't think  there's any compromise in that  relationship speaking of his treatment  senator you on the Intelligence  Committee have had a good close eye and  on the work done by the US intelligence  agencies you've also had a close eye on  what the Russians have been up to I  wanted to get your valuation of what the  president said in Helsinki he said  referring to both Vladimir Putin and the  US intelligence agencies I have  confidence in both parties given your  experience looking at both parties do  you have confidence in both parties I  have absolute confidence in what is  happening in the US intelligence  agencies and what they're doing and how  they're trying to be able to do it they  work very hard to protect the privacy of  Americans but be very aggressive about  trying to pick up information about our  adversaries any attacks they may have  coming towards us so I by far trust more  our US intelligence agencies are you  frustrated with the verbal gymnastics  that we've seen the last couple of days  where would means wouldn't no means no  more questions as opposed to the answer  to a question what are you thinking  about this whole tone that we're  watching as it all plays out for the  last couple of days well it is obviously  odd to be able to watch as we walk  through this for the last several days  on it but I would say I watch actions as  I do more than words on anything when I  know what's happening with the part of  homeland security here and what they're  doing to protect our election systems  what's happening in the intelligence  community and what they're doing in our  systems that's extremely important our  DHS has been by far more prepared for  the 2018 than what they were for the  2016 elections security clearances in  each state trying to be able to work  through the process to protect the  elections we have sanctions on the  Internet research agency that's a  Russian group that tried to interfere in  our social media I would certainly hope  the administration would put sanctions  on all those entities that were involved  from the most recent indictment so  that's how are you feeling about the  actions that's good you're watching the  actions how are you feeling about the  actions of the president over the last  couple of days does anything concern you  I think the president's not careful in  his words in the way that he actually  talked about things but that's no grand  shock that  been true all the way through the  election time period in the campaign all  the way to president now that he's not  careful in how he says things and that  sometimes gets misunderstood and when  you're the president United States  everyone watches every single word and  how you say it which is important  because it does affect the world senator  Lankford Graham to leave it there thank  you you bet thank you
 

